---
/**
 * The release's one screening player: a stage, a transport, the line that says what is on,
 * and the list of the other videos. One component, one border, the same panel the album
 * player is — because it is the same panel. Everything from .player-now down is the shared
 * chrome in global.css, and src/scripts/media-queue.ts drives both.
 *
 * It comes in two shapes and they are one component on purpose. Eight of the thirty-one
 * videos are too large for the pipeline to convert, and the archive has a still of each and
 * nothing else; three releases are made up entirely of those. A release with something to
 * play gets the whole transport, and it goes honestly inactive on the items that cannot —
 * the stage is a <video>, so a poster with no source IS the still, and one element covers
 * both states. A release with nothing to play gets the reel: the same stage, the same queue,
 * previous and next, and NO play button and NO bar, because there is nothing to start and
 * nothing to seek inside and a control that cannot do what it says is worse than no control.
 * Two components would have been two of everything for the sake of one missing row of it.
 *
 * The stage is not a second element beside the player. It IS the player: one
 * <video data-screen-video>, mounted in the same .screening / .screening-stage / mat / dither
 * the rows already use, so a film plays inside the exact frame it was sitting in. The album
 * panel's own <audio> is the same single element and simply takes no space, Chrome's
 * user-agent sheet giving audio:not([controls]) display:none.
 *
 * The list is not written here. ItemRows renders exactly one <ul class="item-rows">, and
 * src/scripts/video-queue.ts moves that list into the empty [data-screen-queue] slot below
 * once this panel is on screen and wired. That is deliberate: the rows carry the per-video
 * view and download links, the numbering and the poster, and a second copy written here would
 * either lose them or double the list.
 *
 * Served hidden and revealed only by that script. Nothing in here works without it, which is
 * why the list keeps its own <video controls> per row until the panel is live — with the
 * script absent the list simply stays where the server put it and the page is what it always
 * was. The `hidden` attribute is load-bearing: global.css carries
 * `[hidden] { display: none !important }` because a scoped rule here would otherwise tie with
 * the UA sheet and show a dead player to a visitor with no JavaScript.
 *
 * preload="none" on the stage, the same as on every one of the 31 rows: the clocks and the
 * bar's range open unknown and are filled in from the element the first time a video is
 * actually loaded. Nothing here is allowed to fetch a byte before someone presses play — the
 * poster the script cues onto the stage is the row's own still, which the queue is already
 * showing, so the stage arrives with a picture and no request of its own. That holds for the
 * reel too, and it is the point of it: the eight files it stands for are 155 MB to 2 GB, they
 * are not hosted here, and nothing this panel does may ask for one.
 */

interface Props {
  /** Names the release's single player, the same label the list of videos carries. */
  labelHe: string;
  /** What the panel is cued to before the script has run a line. */
  firstVideoNameHe: string;
  /** How many videos the queue holds, which the line above it says out loud. */
  videoCount: number;
  /** How many of those the site can actually play. Zero makes this a reel. */
  playableCount: number;
  /** Whether the one the panel opens cued to is among them, which the status line says. */
  firstVideoPlayable: boolean;
}

const { labelHe, firstVideoNameHe, videoCount, playableCount, firstVideoPlayable } = Astro.props;

/* No play button and no bar. The script is held to the same shape by this attribute — see
   REEL_ATTRIBUTE in media-queue.ts — so the markup and the wiring cannot disagree about
   which parts exist. */
const reel = playableCount === 0;

/*
 * What the panel says about itself before anything has been pressed. Three situations and
 * three lines, because a release the site plays in full, a release it plays in part and a
 * release it only has pictures of are three different pieces of news.
 */
const noteHe = reel
  ? `אז יש פה ${videoCount} סרטונים, וכולם גדולים מדי בשביל לשבת פה. מה כן יש? התמונה, האורך, והקובץ עצמו נפתח בלשונית חדשה. כי ככה.`
  : playableCount === videoCount
    ? `אז יש פה ${videoCount} סרטונים. לחצו על אחד, או שבו ותנו להם לרוץ אחד אחרי השני. פופקורן לא סיפקנו.`
    : `אז יש פה ${videoCount} סרטונים. ${playableCount} מהם מתנגנים כאן, והשאר גדולים מדי בשביל לשבת פה — מהם יש תמונה, ואת הקובץ עצמו פותחים בלשונית חדשה.`;
---

<div class="screening-player" data-media-panel data-screen-player data-reel={reel ? '' : undefined} hidden>
  {/* The mount, exactly as a row builds it: a frame on a 1rem mat, and the mat is what pays
      first when the panel is narrower than the picture. Nothing is ever upscaled here — see
      the .screening block in global.css, which argues that number at length, and the reel
      rule at the foot of this file, which argues the other one. */}
  <div class="screening stage-mount">
    <div class="screening-stage mat dither">
      {/* No controls: the panel around it is the control. */}
      <video class="item-player" data-screen-video preload="none" aria-label={labelHe}></video>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="player-body">
    <p class="player-now" role="status">
      {/* Cued, not playing: the script swaps this the first time the release actually starts,
          so the line never claims a video is running before one is. This is also the only
          live region on the page, which is how the queue moving on by itself — a change no
          control made — reaches a screen reader at all. */}
      <span data-screen-now-label>{firstVideoPlayable ? 'מוכן להקרנה:' : 'גדול מדי בשביל לשבת פה:'}</span>{' '}
      <bdi dir="auto" data-screen-now-name>{firstVideoNameHe}</bdi>
    </p>

    {/* One LTR instrument inside an RTL page, the same call .mono makes for filenames and
        sizes: a position inside a duration is a number line, and its two clocks read
        0:00 on the left and the total on the right whatever the page direction is. */}
    <div class="player-controls">
      {/* Left out entirely where nothing on the queue can play. A bar over a still would have
          no position to show and no length to show it in, and serving one that is permanently
          aria-disabled is still serving a bar that will never work. The reel keeps no clock
          anywhere, so a video's length is read off its own row in the queue below. */}
      {
        !reel && (
          <div class="player-seek">
            <span class="mono player-clock" data-screen-elapsed>0:00</span>
            <input
              class="player-range"
              type="range"
              min="0"
              max="0"
              step="1"
              value="0"
              aria-label="איפה אנחנו בסרטון"
              aria-valuetext="0:00"
              aria-disabled="true"
              data-screen-seek
            />
            <span class="mono player-clock" data-screen-total>--:--</span>
          </div>
        )
      }

      {/* Previous and next are the reel's whole transport, and they are not a consolation:
          they change which still is on the stage, which is the only thing there is to change
          about a release the site has pictures of and no films of. */}
      <div class="player-transport">
        <button class="player-button" type="button" aria-label="הסרטון הקודם" aria-disabled="true" data-screen-prev>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path fill="currentColor" d="M7 5h2.5v14H7zM19 5v14l-8.5-7z"></path>
          </svg>
        </button>
        {
          !reel && (
            /* Served already refusing where the first video is one of the ones we do not
               host. The script re-states this on every select; the server states it once so
               the button is never briefly offering something it cannot do. */
            <button
              class="player-button player-toggle"
              type="button"
              aria-label="נגן"
              aria-disabled={firstVideoPlayable ? 'false' : 'true'}
              data-screen-toggle
            >
              <svg class="icon-play" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="26" height="26" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                <path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z" />
              </svg>
              <svg class="icon-pause" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="26" height="26" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                <path fill="currentColor" d="M7 5h3.5v14H7zM13.5 5H17v14h-3.5z" />
              </svg>
            </button>
          )
        }
        <button class="player-button" type="button" aria-label="הסרטון הבא" data-screen-next>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path fill="currentColor" d="M14.5 5H17v14h-2.5zM5 5l8.5 7L5 19z"></path>
          </svg>
        </button>

        {
          !reel && (
            /*
             * How big the film is, which is a question only the visitor gets to answer.
             *
             * Every transcode is 320x240, and this stylesheet spends a page arguing that
             * nothing may be shown larger than it is — .release-cover refuses 2.3x, and the
             * stage is 320px precisely so that 22 of the 23 play at 1:1. None of that is
             * contradicted here, because that rule governs what the site IMPOSES. A control
             * someone presses is the opposite: it is them deciding they would rather have the
             * picture big than have it sharp, which is a trade they are allowed to make and we
             * are not allowed to make for them. So 1:1 stays the default and both of these are
             * off until pressed.
             *
             * Left out of a reel entirely, and that is a decision about the RELEASE rather
             * than about the moment. A reel's stage is 220px because that is the true size of
             * the only thing it will ever hold, which is the same argument these two would be
             * spending; and the file the still stands for is one click away on Drive at its
             * own full size, which is the honest route to bigger there.
             *
             * They do NOT go aria-disabled on a still the way the play button does, and the
             * difference is what each one acts on. The play button acts on playback, and a
             * still has none. These act on the STAGE, which is holding something either way —
             * and a control that had switched itself off while its own state was on would be a
             * mode the visitor could enter and not leave, which is worse than an upscaled
             * still they asked for. So in a mixed release they stay live throughout, and a
             * poster in the wide frame is pixel-doubled like everything else in it.
             */
            <div class="player-view">
              <button
                class="player-button"
                type="button"
                aria-label="מצב אולם"
                aria-pressed="false"
                data-screen-theater
              >
                {/* A wide frame. Stroked for the same reason the queue's still handle is:
                    a filled outline needs its inside to be a hole. */}
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                  <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M3 7h18v10H3z" />
                </svg>
              </button>

              {/* Served hidden and revealed by the script only where the browser will actually
                  go fullscreen. A button that does nothing is the thing this panel has spent
                  its whole design removing. */}
              <button
                class="player-button"
                type="button"
                aria-label="מסך מלא"
                aria-pressed="false"
                data-screen-fullscreen
                hidden
              >
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                  <path
                    fill="currentColor"
                    d="M4 9V4h5v2H6v3H4zM15 4h5v5h-2V6h-3V4zM4 15h2v3h3v2H4v-5zM18 15h2v5h-5v-2h3v-3z"
                  />
                </svg>
              </button>
            </div>
          )
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  {/* The queue's own plate, spanning the panel under the stage and the transport. It is
      served empty and stays empty without JavaScript: the script moves the rendered
      <ul class="item-rows"> in here, so there is only ever one copy of the list and every
      per-video link on it survives the move untouched. */}
  <div class="player-list">
    <p class="player-list-note">
      {noteHe}
    </p>
    {/* Its own scrollport: the longest release here has 15 videos, and a queue that long has
        to run inside the player instead of dragging it down the page. */}
    <div class="player-queue" data-screen-queue></div>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /*
   * Stage beside the controls once there is room for both, stacked before that. The boundary
   * is 681px, which is not a new number: it is the width at which global.css already puts a
   * video row's name beside its frame rather than under it, and a panel is that same pairing
   * one size up. Under it the stage takes the panel's full width and caps itself at the
   * mount's 356px, so the film never grows either way.
   *
   * The whole card stops at 46rem for the same reason the album player does: a player is as
   * wide as a player needs and no wider, and the queue inside it is a name and two links, not
   * a table.
   */
  .screening-player {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(0.7rem, 2vw, 1.2rem);
    max-width: 46rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
    padding: clamp(0.7rem, 2vw, 1rem);
    border: 1px solid var(--stroke);
    border-radius: var(--radius-s);
    background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 8%, var(--raise));
  }

  /* The mount is already `width: min(100%, 356px)` and already carries its own hairline and
     radius, so there is nothing to restate here — only where it sits. */
  .stage-mount {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  /*
   * ---------- the reel's stage ----------
   *
   * 220px, not 320px, and that is the same argument global.css makes rather than an exception
   * to it: a frame is the size of what is actually in it, and nothing in this release will
   * ever be 320px wide. The 23 transcodes are 320 across, which is why the stage is; the 27
   * stills are 220, and standing one in a 320 frame is the 1.45x upscale the .screening block
   * exists to refuse. 220 is what the poster IS.
   *
   * A MIXED release keeps 320, and the same file already blessed the 1.45x there: a still
   * standing in behind the controls of a frame that also plays films has to be the frame the
   * films are. It is only when there are no films that the stills stop being pre-roll and
   * become the content, and the frame follows the content.
   *
   * Scoped rather than parameterised into global.css: the three numbers below are this
   * panel's, and the .screening block's own 320 goes on meaning what it has always meant.
   */
  .screening-player[data-reel] .stage-mount {
    width: min(100%, calc(220px + 2rem + 2px));
  }

  .screening-player[data-reel] .screening-stage {
    padding: max(0px, calc((100% - 220px) / 2));
  }

  .screening-player[data-reel] .item-player {
    max-width: 220px;
  }

  /* Five controls where the album player has three, and at the 681px boundary the column they
     share is 252px against the 241px they want. Ten pixels is not a margin, so the row is told
     to wrap rather than left to overflow the panel: the view pair drops to a line of its own,
     still at the far end, and the transport keeps its shape. */
  .player-transport {
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    row-gap: 0.4rem;
  }

  /* The view controls sit at the far end of the control strip, away from the transport: one
     group decides which film, the other decides how big it is. .player-controls is LTR, so
     the inline start is the left and auto pushes them to the right end of the row. */
  .player-view {
    margin-inline-start: auto;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
  }

  /*
   * ---------- the two enlarged states ----------
   *
   * Both are opt-in, both keep the film inside a dark ground with object-fit: contain, so a
   * 4:3 source on a 16:9 screen letterboxes rather than stretching, and both scale it with
   * image-rendering: pixelated.
   *
   * That last one is the whole difference between offering this and ruining it. A 320x240
   * capture at 3x or 4.5x through the default bilinear filter is mush — the failure this
   * stylesheet calls a smear where it refuses 2.3x for covers. Nearest-neighbour at the same
   * size is not a compromise between the two: it is pixel-doubling, which is what 2003 footage
   * out of a shareware studio actually looked like on the machines it was made for. It reads
   * as a decision rather than as a fault, which is the only way an upscale is allowed to read
   * here. Where an engine does not support it on video the picture is simply the smooth
   * upscale it would have been, so nothing depends on it.
   *
   * The dither goes off in both. It is licensed on a mat and in the base of a poster, and a
   * letterbox bar the height of a screen is neither — the same call video.item-player already
   * makes about its own bars in global.css.
   *
   * No transition on either. The site animates a border colour and an opacity and nothing
   * else, and a panel that grew into the column over 200ms would be the one animation on it;
   * global.css already resets every duration under prefers-reduced-motion regardless.
   */

  /* Theater: the panel gives up its 46rem and takes the content column, and the two-column
     grid folds so the stage gets all of it. Capped at 70vh so a 4:3 film in a 1180px column
     cannot be 885px tall and push its own controls off the screen; past that cap the box
     stops being 4:3 and the film letterboxes inside it, which is what the mat is for. */
  .screening-player[data-theater] {
    max-width: none;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  }

  .screening-player[data-theater] .stage-mount {
    width: 100%;
  }

  .screening-player[data-theater] .screening-stage {
    display: grid;
    padding: 0;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    max-height: 70vh;
    background-image: none;
  }

  /* Fullscreen after theater deliberately: the two are the same shape of selector, so when
     both are set the larger state wins on source order. */
  .screening-player[data-fullscreen] {
    max-width: none;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-rows: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    align-content: stretch;
    height: 100%;
    margin: 0;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 0;
  }

  /* The queue is a scrollport 26rem tall; on a screen given over to one film it is the part
     that is not the film. Previous and next still step the release, which is the whole of what
     the queue was for while it is gone. */
  .screening-player[data-fullscreen] .player-list {
    display: none;
  }

  .screening-player[data-fullscreen] .stage-mount {
    width: 100%;
    min-height: 0;
    height: 100%;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 0;
  }

  /* aspect-ratio and the 70vh cap are undone rather than left standing: pressing fullscreen
     while theater is on would otherwise keep the stage at 70% of a screen given over to it. */
  .screening-player[data-fullscreen] .screening-stage {
    display: grid;
    padding: 0;
    height: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: auto;
    max-height: none;
    background-image: none;
  }

  /* One rule for both states, and the one place the 320px cap is lifted — by a press, and
     never otherwise. */
  .screening-player[data-theater] .item-player,
  .screening-player[data-fullscreen] .item-player {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    max-width: none;
    aspect-ratio: auto;
    object-fit: contain;
    image-rendering: pixelated;
  }

  @media (min-width: 681px) {
    .screening-player {
      /* 356px is the mount's own width, stated so the grid reserves the frame rather than
         waiting for the video element to have a size. At the 681px boundary the panel has
         about 617px inside its padding, which leaves the controls 241px — clear of the
         ~157px the two clocks, their gaps and a usable bar bottom out at. */
      grid-template-columns: calc(320px + 2rem + 2px) minmax(0, 1fr);
      align-items: center;
    }

    /* 256px of mount, so the reel's controls get 352px of the same 622px — and there is less
       to fit in them, the transport being two buttons and no bar. */
    .screening-player[data-reel] {
      grid-template-columns: calc(220px + 2rem + 2px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
